A spring wedding is a bet on light: lengthening days, a garden coming into bloom from one week to the next and guests emerging from winter eager to celebrate. It is also a season that changes fast, between a still-chilly end of March and a June that already feels like summer. We have hosted spring weddings for years at the Château de la Huberdière, near Amboise, and here is what we advise couples before they set their date.

## Why choose a spring wedding?

A spring wedding offers mild weather without the heat of summer, long days for photographs and a setting that blooms all by itself. In Touraine, May afternoons hover around 20 °C according to Météo-France averages for Tours, which lets you hold a ceremony outdoors without guests hunting for shade.

Spring also has a practical advantage. Your guests are rested, they do not yet have three weddings to attend over the summer, and their holidays are not booked. A May weekend becomes a first getaway of the year for them, not one more obligation in July.

There is a downside: the weather stays unsettled, especially in March and April. A spring wedding should therefore be prepared with two plans, and we come back to this below.

![A bed of pink and fuchsia peonies in full bloom in the château garden](/images/inline/jardin-fleurs/jardin-fleurs-04.webp "The garden peonies bloom in May. They often decide the palette of couples marrying that month.")

## March, April, May or June: which month to choose?

Choose May for the balance of mild weather, light and a garden in bloom, June for the most reliable weather, April for a free date and a more flexible budget. March remains an indoor wedding that makes the most of the grounds for photographs.

### March, the season that is just beginning

In March, the grounds are barely turning green and the evenings are still cold. It is a wedding designed around the drawing rooms, with the grounds as a backdrop. Dates are free and suppliers available, as for a [winter wedding](/en/blog/winter-wedding-chateau-loire).

### April, the date for couples who plan ahead

April offers around 16 °C in the afternoon and showers that give no warning. The light is beautiful, the wisteria flowers on the walls, rates stay softer than in June. An outdoor ceremony is worth trying as long as you have a fallback ready to go.

### May, the heart of the season

May is our favourite month: the garden is at its peak, the peonies come out, and the pool has been heated since April for guests who stay on Sunday. It is also the month of the long weekends, and so of the most contested dates.

### June, the spring that feels like summer

June approaches 23 °C in the afternoon and brings the longest evenings of the year. Dinner can end outdoors. It is the safe choice for the weather, and that is why June Saturdays go more than a year ahead.

## The May 2027 long weekends: an opportunity and a trap

In 2027, 1 May and 8 May (French bank holidays) fall on a Saturday, Ascension is Thursday 6 May and Whit Monday (Pentecost Monday, a French bank holiday) is 17 May. These dates allow a wedding over three or four days, but they are the first to go, with venues and suppliers alike.

An Ascension long weekend suits a wedding in several stages very well: arrival on Thursday, ceremony on Friday or Saturday, brunch on Sunday. Your guests only need to take one or two days off. In return, book venue, caterer and photographer at least twelve months ahead, and check each one’s terms for a bank-holiday Saturday such as 1 or 8 May. Our [wedding planning timeline](/en/blog/wedding-planning-timeline) gives the order of bookings month by month.

## Which flowers for a spring wedding?

For a spring wedding, choose seasonal flowers: tulips, ranunculus and anemones in March and April, peonies, lily of the valley and the first roses in May, garden roses and light foliage in June. They cost less, last longer and go with the garden setting.

Our advice is to let the venue carry part of the decoration. In spring, the grounds do the work: an avenue of wisteria, a bed of roses, a freshly mown lawn. A local florist knows what is blooming that week, so ask what will look beautiful on your date rather than imposing a photo found online.

![The park lawn in spring, a rose bush in flower and loungers in the shade of the trees](/images/inline/jardin-fleurs/jardin-fleurs-03.webp "The grounds in late spring. Sunday guests spend the whole day here.")

## The rain plan, to be decided at booking

A spring wedding is prepared with a covered space able to seat all your guests, chosen when you book and not the night before. A good plan B is not a makeshift fallback, it is a second setting that you love as much as the first.

At the château, the Orangerie and the drawing rooms serve as the fallback for the ceremony as well as for dinner. Together we set a decision time on the morning of the wedding, usually late morning, to give the caterer and the teams time to set up. Once that time has passed, there is no going back: nothing wears a couple’s nerves down more than watching the weather until 3 pm.

Plan for the evening chill too. Even in May, the temperature drops quickly after 9 pm in Touraine. A few blankets on the chairs for the drinks reception and dinner indoors are enough for everyone.

![Vaulted reception room of the château, round tables with white linen and gilded chairs with burgundy seats](/images/inline/mariage/mariage-08.webp "The vaulted room, ready for dinner. It is the one that hosts the party when spring does not play along.")

## A humanist ceremony in the grounds

Spring is the best season for an outdoor humanist ceremony: the late-afternoon light is soft, the grounds are green and guests can stand without suffering from the heat. A ceremony between 4 pm and 5 pm in May enjoys a low light that is ideal for photographs.

The spot in the grounds is chosen on a visit: under the tall trees for shade, facing the pond for the view, on the lawn for space. Our article on the [humanist ceremony in a château park](/en/blog/humanist-wedding-ceremony-chateau) details the running order, the celebrant and the sound system.

## A spring wedding at the Château de la Huberdière

For a spring wedding near Amboise, the Château de la Huberdière is hired in full for the weekend, for 30 to 150 guests, 2 hrs 20 from Paris. The estate has 14 hectares of enclosed grounds, a pond and 10 bedrooms that sleep up to 21 people on site.

Exclusive hire for a weekend runs from 4,000 € to 8,000 € depending on the season, and the item-by-item breakdown is in our article on the [cost of a château wedding](/en/blog/chateau-wedding-cost). You choose your caterer; when we provide the meal, corkage is 8 € per bottle. For couples who want support, our coordination package at 2,500 € takes on the role of a wedding planner, provided by the people who live on site.

Loved ones who stay on after the party can extend their stay in our [charming hotel rooms](/en/boutique-hotel-amboise), and Sunday is often spent beside the lap pool.

Spring 2027 is booking now, and the May weekends go first. Discover the venue and [ask for a proposal for your wedding](/en/chateau-wedding-loire): we reply within 24 hours.
